Split out of karr #113, measured with the same corpus (sops 3.13.3 at /tmp/sops).

karr #113 narrowed the non-finite guard for the UNENCRYPTED slot, where the
leaf reaches the document as a plain token. The ENCRYPTED slot is untouched
and still refuses every non-finite float -- now from an explicit croak in
Encrypted::encrypt_value rather than from assert_representable.

MEASURED, guard bypassed in a scratch process, twelve %GO_CONSTANT tokens as
dualvars plus three bare NVs, encrypted slot:

  YAML   sops -d exit 0  in every row -- the wire is ENC[...,type:float] with
         the plaintext +Inf / -Inf / NaN, and sops writes .inf / -.inf / .nan
         back out. The string half plays no part: dualvar(+Inf, "banana")
         gives the same document as a bare 9**9**9.
  JSON   sops -d exit 4 in every row -- "Error dumping file: Error marshaling
         to json: Error encoding value" (Go cannot marshal a non-finite
         float64), so there is no JSON document for it, encrypted or not.

So the refusal is right for JSON and too wide for YAML, and the two cannot be
told apart where it lives: encrypt_value is format-blind, and so is
assert_representable, which it is reached from. Widening it needs the document
format at the leaf, which today is known only in File::SOPS (_encrypt_tree,
_compute_mac) -- see the same wall karr #113 hit for the unencrypted slot.

Consequence today: rotate of a sops-written YAML document whose ENCRYPTED slot
holds a .inf still croaks. The leaf decrypts to a bare Perl infinity (no token,
karr #113s gate cannot help it), and re-encrypting it would produce exactly the
document sops itself writes.

Wire lane. Needs the binary to prove, and probably an argument through
File::SOPS first.
